  "tex": [
    "A broken line consists of three consecutive segments of lengths $a,b,c$, measured in whole units.\n\nShow that grouping the first two lengths or the last two lengths gives the same total:\n\\[\n(a+b)+c=a+(b+c).\n\\]\n",
    "Exercise \\exref{Notebook26,1.2.1} gives $a+(b+c)=(a+b)+c$. Reverse that equality. Thus both groupings give the same length.\n",
    "Apply \\texttt{DoYourExercise.Notebook26.ex_1_2_1} to $a,b,c$. The theorem from \\exref{Notebook26,1.2.1} gives $a+(b+c)=(a+b)+c$.\n\nReverse this equality with \\texttt{.symm} to obtain the goal.\n",
    "Let $n$ be a natural number. Prove that\n\\[\nn+0=n.\n\\]\n",
    "Adding zero leaves a natural number unchanged. Therefore $n+0=n$.\n",
    "Let $n$ be a natural number. The goal is $n+0=n$.\n\nApply the library theorem \\texttt{Nat.add_zero} to $n$. It gives exactly this equality, so the goal is closed.\n",
    "Let $n,m$ be natural numbers. Prove that\n\\[\n(n+0)+m=n+m.\n\\]\n",
    "By \\exref{1.1.1}, we have $n+0=n$. Substituting this equality into the left-hand side gives\n\\[\n(n+0)+m=n+m,\n\\]\nas required.\n",
    "Let $n,m$ be natural numbers. Rewrite $n+0$ to $n$ using the previously proved theorem \\texttt{ex_1_1_1} from \\exref{1.1.1}.\n\nBoth sides now read $n+m$. The rewrite closes the equality goal by reflexivity.\n",
    "Let $a,b,c$ be natural numbers. Show that\n\\[\na+(b+c)=(a+b)+c.\n\\]\n",
    "Addition of natural numbers is associative, so $(a+b)+c=a+(b+c)$. Reverse this equality to obtain the required statement.\n",
    "Apply \\texttt{Nat.add_assoc} to $a,b,c$. It gives $(a+b)+c=a+(b+c)$.\n\nReverse the equality using \\texttt{.symm}. The result has exactly the type of the goal.\n",
    "Let $n,m,k$ be natural numbers. Suppose that $n+k=m+k$. Prove that $n=m$.\n",
    "Let $n,m$ be natural numbers. Prove that $n\\leq n+m$.\n"
  ]
